#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca1a126-0de5-5dd4-9dfc-dc91ccd2ea49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309084} {:answer/id #uuid "7faf511e-bf00-51de-a619-877bd56f6290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309085} {:answer/id #uuid "35d7cc02-ae0f-588e-8ff0-cff145761007" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309086} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed49497-1a24-5ebc-a9cd-d73701101f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309087} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b64c66-f74d-5d76-bfae-b5f783d4cc9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ed49497-1a24-5ebc-a9cd-d73701101f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309087} :answer-field/id #uuid "de7b849a-ca96-5579-b91e-f85a7aac7cd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "272ae442-0eb1-5b16-aba6-999ab95c4bf6" :question/math-academy-id "q-21504" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=-6{x}^{2}+36x-48,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=3$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(3)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=3$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=-6{x}^{2}+36x-48,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-12x+36,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12x+36 & =0 \\\\ -12(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have a critical point at $x=3.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-12,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=3$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(3)=-12<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=3,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=3$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(3)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=3\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44aa782b-d055-5d93-a386-a8b49b828205" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309091} {:answer/id #uuid "ee4a7a56-3a5c-58a5-be69-0682d73bde4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309092} {:answer/id #uuid "c6cff171-ef3f-5d60-a9f7-a8307991cad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309093} {:answer/id #uuid "be13dfee-3409-5074-a64d-603766a3f42c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309094} {:answer/id #uuid "86953212-600f-5b8d-ad71-5809e02af13e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44aa782b-d055-5d93-a386-a8b49b828205" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309091} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ec32751-2902-5efd-b882-aad408b5f4c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11a248cf-66d8-5ef2-be89-74a7b6dbe4ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-21814" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=4{x}^{5}+5{x}^{4}-10{x}^{2}-20x+30.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(-1)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-1)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=-1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=4{x}^{5}+5{x}^{4}-10{x}^{2}-20x+30,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=20({x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-x-1).\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=20(4{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =20(4(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =20(-4+3-1) \\\\  & =-40<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6c1e0e1-d791-5714-ba86-1da24f9494e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309098} {:answer/id #uuid "7fa13942-2c37-5749-a0d2-3eafc793a16b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309099} {:answer/id #uuid "bc82bcdc-1f77-5f0d-89ef-3d71fc054705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309100} {:answer/id #uuid "82eb78a7-6574-57cd-86e8-e94dd5b4f41a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309101} {:answer/id #uuid "29b53951-f44b-5069-8357-9d7af07393ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fa13942-2c37-5749-a0d2-3eafc793a16b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309099} :answer-field/id #uuid "bca72a99-5383-5f75-9603-5becf96eaaa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "686a27c2-b67a-56d7-83b5-6def2ee11b09" :question/math-academy-id "q-22080" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)={x}^{5}-5{x}^{3}+15,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=\\sqrt{3}$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(\\sqrt{3})<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=\\sqrt{3}$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=\\sqrt{3}$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)={x}^{5}-5{x}^{3}+15,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=5{x}^{4}-15{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{4}-15{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-3) & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}(x+\\sqrt{3})(x-\\sqrt{3}) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{3},0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=\\pm \\sqrt{3},0.$ We're interested in the critical point $x=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=20{x}^{3}-30x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(\\sqrt{3})=20(\\sqrt{3})^{3}-30\\sqrt{3}=30\\sqrt{3}>0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is positive at $x=\\sqrt{3},$ so this critical point is a relative *minimum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=\\sqrt{3}$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(\\sqrt{3})<0\\,\\times $\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *minimum* at $x=\\sqrt{3}\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86648d9c-87f0-512b-8666-615f45ab673d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309105} {:answer/id #uuid "26248452-dfee-5abe-8134-9c0e3337a763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309106} {:answer/id #uuid "107b2a34-5585-523b-8d78-8961db0c3712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309107} {:answer/id #uuid "738917c7-e8af-57b0-9dc3-5482f09d0d8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309108} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc5787d-23a5-52a2-8acb-03de18913649" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26248452-dfee-5abe-8134-9c0e3337a763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309106} :answer-field/id #uuid "14e030bd-d590-53f3-a5f0-5c3420fa66c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1df74f84-f65d-533a-a7f9-fc6a2bfb54bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-22187" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+3x+3.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2})=0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+3x+3,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12{x}^{2}-12x+3=3(2x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=24x-12.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =24(\\frac{1}{2})-12=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. These points are the solution of the equation ${f}^{′}(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the only stationary point is $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nLet's choose auxiliary points from each side of the stationary point and evaluate the first derivative at them. We take the auxiliary points $x=0,1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =3(2\\cdot 0-1)^{2}=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =3(2\\cdot 1-1)^{2}=3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the first derivative does not change the sign at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$, we conclude that $f(x)$ has no relative extrema at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309110 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ad0de3b-a284-5549-8697-f3cc555ea347" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309112} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d07aca-9305-5888-8e03-a213c0eccdda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309113} {:answer/id #uuid "0f399f28-cb00-5eb8-bbc8-4da5cb14c9f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309114} {:answer/id #uuid "b665cfd4-d5d0-5a91-a926-e27580681ee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309115} {:answer/id #uuid "d347042a-08c1-5ed7-a04b-6222e8ce335f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b665cfd4-d5d0-5a91-a926-e27580681ee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309115} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bbdbf0a-ffd0-51ea-bf44-fe626c6a89c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0df64e1e-8ab1-53de-b065-e9667a06e344" :question/math-academy-id "q-22561" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}).$ Given that ${f}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=0$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\frac{2-2{x}^{2}}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =\\frac{2-2(0)^{2}}{{(1+{0}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =2>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
